# Exercise: Reading Environment Variables
#
# `os.getenv` looks up an environment variable. It returns *two*
# values — the variable's value and a Bool flag that tells you
# whether the variable was actually set:
#
# Stack effect: ( name -- value present? )
#   value     : String — the variable's value, or "" when missing
#   present?  : Bool   — true when the variable was set,
#                        false otherwise
#
# Branch on the flag with `if`. The idiom is the same shape as
# the one for `file.slurp`:
#
#   "HOME" os.getenv [ ...use HOME... ]
#                    [ drop ...fallback... ]
#                    if
#
# Heads up: there is no `os.setenv`. Environment variables in
# Seq are read-only — set them in the calling shell, not from
# inside your program.
#
# Your task: write `getenv-or` — given a variable name and a
# fallback string, return the variable's value when set, or the
# fallback when it is not.
#
# Stack hint: after `swap os.getenv` your stack is
# ( fallback value present? ). On true: keep value, drop fallback.
# On false: drop the empty value, keep fallback.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: getenv-or ( String String -- String )
    # Your code here
    nip
;

: test-fallback ( -- )
    "SEQLINGS_DEFINITELY_UNSET_4242" "fallback" getenv-or

    # Do not edit below this line
    "fallback" test.assert-eq-str
;
